                <a
                  class="link"
                  href="https://strom.physics.northwestern.edu/"
                  target="_blank"
                >
                  Strom Research Group ↗
                </a>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>

          <article class="project">
            <div class="number">02</div>

            <div class="project-content">
              <div class="tags">
                <span class="tag">Keck Observatory</span>
                <span class="tag">MOSFIRE</span>
                <span class="tag">Observing</span>
              </div>

              <h3>From Evanston to Hawaiʻi: deep spectra with Keck</h3>

              <p>
                I participated in a Keck/MOSFIRE observing run on Mauna Kea,
                collecting deep near-infrared spectra of distant galaxies. The
                experience connected the finished spectra on my computer to the
                masks, exposures, calibrations, and observing conditions that
                produce them.
              </p>

              <div class="project-details">
                <div>
                  <strong>Instrument</strong>
                  <span>Keck I’s MOSFIRE near-infrared spectrograph</span>
                </div>

                <div>
                  <strong>Strategy</strong>
                  <span>Slit masks observing approximately 20–25 galaxies at once</span>
                </div>

                <div>
                  <strong>Purpose</strong>
                  <span>
                    Expand the comparison sample and improve measurements of
                    faint emission features
                  </span>
                </div>
              </div>

              <div class="links">
                <a class="link" href="Expo2025.pdf" target="_blank">
                  Project presentation ↗
                </a>

                <a
                  class="link"
                  href="https://www.keckobservatory.org/about/telescopes-instrumentation/"
                  target="_blank"
                >
                  Keck instrumentation ↗
                </a>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>

          <article class="project">
            <div class="number">03</div>

            <div class="project-content">
              <div class="tags">
                <span class="tag">Yale Astronomy</span>
                <span class="tag">Summer 2025</span>
                <span class="tag">JWST</span>
              </div>

              <h3>A spectroscopic analysis of the Little Red Dots</h3>

              <p>
                Little Red Dots are compact, unusually red sources found by JWST
                in the early universe. Working with Professor Meg Urry and
                Alessandro Peca, I searched their spectra for high-ionization
                [Ne V] emission, a potential signature of accreting black holes.
              </p>

              <div class="project-details">
                <div>
                  <strong>Sample</strong>
                  <span>24 candidates selected from JADES and 22 available spectra</span>
                </div>

                <div>
                  <strong>Method</strong>
                  <span>
                    Redshift-aware line searches, local noise estimates, and
                    signal-to-noise measurements
                  </span>
                </div>

                <div>
                  <strong>Result</strong>
                  <span>
                    No individual [Ne V] detection exceeded 3σ; the spectra
                    instead provided quantitative upper limits
                  </span>
                </div>
              </div>

              <details>
                <summary>Read the project abstract</summary>

                <p>
                  Rather than treating a non-detection as no result, I used the
                  spectral noise surrounding each expected line location to
                  calculate upper limits. Those constraints help establish the
                  depth that future observations would need to reach.
                </p>
              </details>

              <div class="links">
                <a
                  class="link"
                  href="Little%20Red%20Dots%20Summer%2025%27%20YALE%20-%20Katarina%20Andrade%20(1).pdf"
                  target="_blank"
